topic("taxes", "Taxes", ["Financial Analysis", "Business Law", "Economics"],
      "How taxes work, filing a return, tax-advantaged accounts and how tax law shapes investing and business decisions.")

sub("income-tax", "How taxes work: types, brackets, deductions and credits", ["FI:067", "EC:072"],
    ["Taxes pay for public goods and services such as roads, schools, defense and Social Security. Governments tax income (federal, most states, some cities), payroll (Social Security and Medicare), purchases (sales and excise taxes), property, estates and gifts, corporate profits and imports (tariffs). A tax is progressive if higher earners pay a larger share of income (the federal income tax), regressive if lower earners pay a larger share (sales taxes) and proportional if everyone pays the same rate.",
     "The federal income tax uses brackets: 10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35% and 37%. Your marginal rate is the rate on your last dollar; your effective rate is total tax divided by income, and it's always lower. Moving into a higher bracket taxes only the dollars above the line, so a raise never lowers take-home pay. Deductions reduce taxable income; credits reduce the tax itself, dollar for dollar, which makes them more valuable."],
    ["Taxable income = gross income - adjustments - standard (or itemized) deduction.",
     "A $1,000 credit saves $1,000. A $1,000 deduction saves $1,000 x your marginal rate ($220 in the 22% bracket).",
     "Refundable credits (like the Earned Income Tax Credit) can produce a refund even if you owe no tax.",
     "Nine states have no tax on wage income, including Texas, Florida and Washington; five have no statewide sales tax (Alaska, Delaware, Montana, New Hampshire, Oregon).",
     "Federal corporate income tax: a flat 21% since 2018."],
    ["Standard deduction for 2026: $16,100 single, $32,200 married filing jointly. For 2027: $16,600 single, $33,200 joint, $24,925 head of household (IRS Rev. Proc. 2026-35, Nov. 2026).",
     "The seven rates stay the same for 2027; bracket thresholds rise about 3.2% for inflation, and the 37% rate starts above $661,375 for single filers (IRS).",
     "The child tax credit is $2,200 per qualifying child under the 2025 tax law, now indexed to inflation (IRS).",
     "For 2025 through 2028, people 65 and older can take an extra $6,000 deduction, phased out at higher incomes (IRS)."],
    ["The IRS now calls the 2025 tax law the 'Working Families Tax Cuts'; it made the 2017 rates permanent and added deductions for tips, overtime, seniors and U.S.-made car loan interest.",
     "A worker who earns $1 over a bracket line pays the higher rate on just that $1, a myth that stops some people from taking raises or overtime."],
    ["A higher bracket applies only to income above the threshold.", "Credits beat deductions of the same size."],
    "\"Moving into the 22% bracket only taxes the dollars above the line. Your raise always means more take-home pay, never less.\"",
    [("Our client is worried a raise will push them into a higher tax bracket. What do you tell them?",
      "That a raise always increases take-home pay. The U.S. uses marginal brackets, so only the dollars above the threshold are taxed at the higher rate. If a single filer earns $1,000 over the 12% line, only that $1,000 is taxed at 22%, an extra $100, not their whole income. Their effective rate barely moves. Take the raise, and send part of it to a 401(k), which lowers taxable income too."),
     ("What's the difference between a tax deduction and a tax credit?",
      "A deduction lowers the income you're taxed on; a credit lowers the tax itself, dollar for dollar. In the 22% bracket, a $1,000 deduction saves $220, but a $1,000 credit saves the full $1,000. That's why the $2,200-per-child tax credit matters so much to young families. We'd make sure our client claims every credit they qualify for before worrying about itemizing.")],
    [IRS, ["Tax Foundation", "https://taxfoundation.org"]])

sub("filing", "Preparing and filing an income tax return", ["FI:074"],
    ["Filing a tax return reports a year's income to the IRS and settles the difference between tax owed and tax already paid through withholding or estimated payments. Most people file Form 1040, due April 15 of the following year. Before filing, gather income forms: a W-2 from each employer, 1099s for freelance work (1099-NEC), interest (1099-INT), dividends (1099-DIV), investment sales (1099-B) and, new for 2025 sales, crypto (1099-DA), plus records of deductions and credits.",
     "Filing status (single, married filing jointly, married filing separately, head of household, qualifying surviving spouse) sets the brackets and standard deduction. A teen with a part-time job usually should file even if not required, to get withheld tax refunded. Self-employed people pay quarterly estimated taxes and 15.3% self-employment tax on net earnings, covering both halves of Social Security and Medicare."],
    ["Forms: W-4 (set withholding), W-2 (wages from employer), 1099 (other income), 1040 (the return), Schedule C (business profit or loss).",
     "An extension gives until October 15 to file, but not to pay; interest and penalties run on unpaid tax from April 15.",
     "Failure-to-file penalty: 5% of unpaid tax per month, up to 25%; failure-to-pay: 0.5% per month (IRS).",
     "Free help: IRS Free File for eligible incomes and Volunteer Income Tax Assistance (VITA) sites.",
     "A big refund means you over-withheld, an interest-free loan to the government; adjust the W-4 to keep more each paycheck."],
    ["Self-employment tax is 15.3% (12.4% Social Security up to the wage base, plus 2.9% Medicare) on net self-employment earnings (IRS).",
     "Brokers must report digital asset sales on the new Form 1099-DA starting with 2025 transactions (IRS)."],
    ["The IRS never makes first contact by text, email or social media, or demands payment by gift card; tax-season scams copy the IRS logo to steal refunds (IRS).",
     "Gig workers on apps like Uber and Etsy receive 1099-K or 1099-NEC forms and must set aside money for taxes, since nothing is withheld."],
    ["An extension extends the time to file, not to pay.", "A refund isn't a bonus; it's your own overpaid money."],
    "\"As a freelancer, set aside about 25% to 30% of each payment in a separate savings account for taxes, and pay estimated tax each quarter so April has no surprises.\"",
    [("Our client just started freelancing. What tax steps should they take?",
      "Three things. First, open a separate account and move 25% to 30% of every payment into it, because no tax is withheld and they'll owe 15.3% self-employment tax plus income tax. Second, pay estimated taxes quarterly to avoid underpayment penalties. Third, track business expenses like software and mileage, which reduce taxable profit on Schedule C. That keeps April from becoming a crisis."),
     ("Our client got a $3,000 refund and wants to celebrate. Any advice?",
      "Celebrate a little, then put most of it to work, and fix the withholding. A $3,000 refund means they lent the government $250 a month interest-free. Updating the W-4 puts that money into each paycheck instead, where it could go automatically to savings. This year's refund could fill the emergency fund or open a Roth IRA, which turns a one-time windfall into lasting progress.")],
    [IRS, ["IRS Free File", "https://www.irs.gov/filing/irs-free-file-do-your-federal-taxes-for-free"]])

sub("tax-advantaged", "Tax-advantaged accounts", ["BL:134", "FI:569"],
    ["Congress uses the tax code to encourage saving for retirement, health care and education. Tax-advantaged accounts either give a tax break now (traditional: contributions are deductible or pre-tax, and withdrawals are taxed later) or later (Roth: contributions are taxed now, and qualified withdrawals are tax-free). Inside both, investments grow without yearly taxes on dividends and gains.",
     "The main accounts are employer plans (401(k), 403(b) for nonprofits and schools, 457 for governments, TSP for federal workers), individual retirement accounts (traditional and Roth IRAs), health savings accounts (HSAs, which are triple tax-advantaged), 529 education plans and, since 2026, Trump Accounts for children. Each has contribution limits, rules on who qualifies and penalties for early or non-qualified withdrawals, usually a 10% penalty plus tax before age 59½ for retirement accounts."],
    ["Order of operations: 401(k) up to the full match, then a Roth IRA, then an HSA if eligible, then more 401(k).",
     "Roth makes sense when your tax rate now is low (students, early career); traditional when your rate now is high.",
     "Roth IRA contributions (not earnings) can be withdrawn anytime without tax or penalty.",
     "HSA: deductible going in, tax-free growth, tax-free out for medical costs; requires a high-deductible health plan; the money is yours forever.",
     "Starting in 2027, SECURE 2.0's Saver's Match will deposit a federal match of up to $1,000 into lower-income savers' retirement accounts."],
    ["401(k) employee limit: $24,500 for 2026 and $25,500 for 2027; catch-up for 50+: $8,000 (2026) and $8,500 (2027); ages 60-63: $11,250 (2026) and $11,750 (2027) (IRS).",
     "IRA limit: $7,500 for 2026 and 2027, plus a $1,100 catch-up at 50+ (IRS).",
     "HSA limit: $4,400 self-only / $8,750 family for 2026; $4,500 / $9,000 for 2027 (IRS Rev. Proc. 2026-24)."],
    ["Fidelity reports that a record number of its 401(k) savers have balances of $1 million or more, almost all built through steady contributions and employer matches over decades.",
     "Federal employees' Thrift Savings Plan offers index funds with expense ratios of a few hundredths of a percent, one of the cheapest plans in the country."],
    ["Traditional = tax break now; Roth = tax break later.", "HSAs don't 'use it or lose it' like FSAs do."],
    "\"Since you're in a low bracket now, a Roth IRA lets you pay tax at 12% today and never pay tax on the growth, even if it grows tenfold.\"",
    [("Should our client choose a Roth or a traditional 401(k)?",
      "It depends on whether their tax rate is lower now or later. Early in a career, in the 10% or 12% bracket, Roth usually wins: they pay a little tax now and every dollar of growth comes out tax-free in retirement. A high earner in the 32% bracket may prefer traditional to cut taxes today. Many clients split contributions between both, which gives tax flexibility later."),
     ("Our client can only save $300 a month. Which account should it go in?",
      "First, enough in the 401(k) to get the full employer match, since that's an instant 50% to 100% return. If any is left, a Roth IRA, where contributions can be withdrawn without penalty in a true emergency. For example, if a 4% contribution earns the full match and costs $200 a month, the other $100 goes to the Roth. Putting the money in the right accounts makes the same $300 work much harder.")],
    [IRS, INV])

sub("tax-investing-biz", "How tax law affects investing and business transactions", ["BL:134"],
    ["Tax law shapes almost every financial decision. For investors, profits on assets held more than a year are long-term capital gains, taxed at 0%, 15% or 20% depending on income; assets held a year or less are taxed as ordinary income at up to 37%. A 3.8% net investment income tax applies to high earners. Interest from municipal bonds is usually free from federal tax. Inherited assets get a 'step-up' in cost basis, which erases capital gains built up during the owner's life.",
     "For businesses, tax rules affect structure and timing. A C corporation pays the 21% corporate tax, and shareholders pay tax again on dividends: double taxation. Pass-through businesses (sole proprietorships, partnerships, S corporations, most LLCs) report profit on owners' personal returns and may deduct up to 20% of qualified business income. Depreciation spreads an asset's cost over its life, and the 2025 law permanently restored 100% bonus depreciation, letting firms deduct qualifying equipment in year one."],
    ["Hold investments over a year when possible to get the lower long-term rate.",
     "Tax-loss harvesting: sell losers to offset gains (up to $3,000 of extra loss against ordinary income each year).",
     "Wash-sale rule: a loss is disallowed if you buy the same or a 'substantially identical' security within 30 days before or after the sale.",
     "Asset location: hold tax-inefficient investments (bonds, REITs) in IRAs and 401(k)s; hold index funds in taxable accounts.",
     "Businesses must file Form 8300 for cash payments over $10,000; online sellers must collect sales tax in states where they meet thresholds (South Dakota v. Wayfair, 2018)."],
    ["Long-term capital gains rates: 0%, 15%, 20%; short-term gains are taxed at ordinary rates up to 37% (IRS).",
     "Federal corporate tax rate: 21% (since the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act).",
     "The 20% qualified business income deduction for pass-through owners was made permanent by the 2025 tax law (IRS)."],
    ["After the 2018 Wayfair decision, states could require out-of-state online sellers to collect sales tax, changing costs for e-commerce businesses.",
     "Companies time big equipment purchases around depreciation rules; 100% bonus depreciation lets a firm deduct a $200,000 machine immediately instead of over several years."],
    ["Short-term = one year or less.", "S corporations avoid double taxation; C corporations face it."],
    "\"Holding this stock just 30 more days moves the gain from your 22% rate to the 15% long-term rate, which saves $70 on every $1,000 of profit.\"",
    [("How would taxes affect the way our client sells investments?",
      "Timing matters a lot. A gain on an investment held a year or less is taxed at ordinary rates, up to 37%, while one held longer gets the 0%, 15% or 20% long-term rate. On a $10,000 gain, that can be the difference between $2,200 and $1,500 of tax for someone in the 22% bracket. We'd also sell losing positions to offset gains, while watching the 30-day wash-sale rule."),
     ("Should our small-business client form an S corporation or stay an LLC?",
      "It depends on profit. A single-member LLC is simple and passes profit through to the owner's return, but all of it faces 15.3% self-employment tax. Once profit is steady, often above $60,000 to $80,000, an S corporation election lets the owner take a reasonable salary and receive the rest as distributions without payroll tax, which can save thousands. It adds payroll and filing costs, so we'd have a CPA run the numbers for their exact situation.")],
    [IRS, SBA])

