topic("insurance", "Insurance & risk management", ["Financial Analysis", "Risk Management", "Strategic Management"],
      "How insurance works, what people and businesses need, the risk management process, enterprise risk management and internal controls.")

sub("insurance-basics", "How insurance works", ["FI:081"],
    ["Insurance transfers risk from a person or business to an insurance company. Many people pay a small, certain cost, the premium, into a pool, and the pool pays the few who suffer a large, uncertain loss. It works because of the law of large numbers: an insurer can't predict who will have a car accident, but it can predict closely how many of a million drivers will. Insurance is for losses you couldn't afford, not for small, predictable costs.",
     "Every policy is a contract with key terms: premium (the price), deductible (what you pay first), coinsurance or copay (your share after the deductible), policy limit (the most it will pay), exclusions (what isn't covered) and beneficiary (who receives a life insurance payout). Insurers underwrite, or price risk, using data like age, driving record, claims history and location. Insurance is regulated by the states, not the federal government."],
    ["Higher deductible = lower premium; choose the highest deductible your emergency fund can cover.",
     "Insurable interest: you can only insure something whose loss would harm you.",
     "Principle of indemnity: insurance restores you to where you were, not better off.",
     "Adverse selection: people who expect claims buy more insurance. Moral hazard: insured people may take more risk.",
     "Insurers transfer their own large risks to reinsurers; they're rated for financial strength by firms like AM Best."],
    ["U.S. insured losses from natural disasters are now commonly over $100 billion a year (Insurance Information Institute and reinsurer reports).",
     "In California and Florida, several large insurers stopped writing new home policies in parts of the state after wildfire and hurricane losses, pushing more homeowners onto state-run insurers of last resort."],
    ["The January 2025 Los Angeles wildfires were among the costliest disasters in U.S. history, with insured losses estimated in the tens of billions of dollars.",
     "Allstate (2022) and State Farm (2023) stopped writing new home policies in California, citing wildfire risk and rebuilding costs."],
    ["The deductible is paid by you, before insurance pays.", "Insurance covers pure risk (loss or no loss), not speculative risk like investing."],
    "\"Insurance is for the losses you couldn't recover from. We'd raise your deductible to $1,000, which your emergency fund can cover, and use the premium savings to buy coverage that really matters.\"",
    [("Why would our client pay for insurance they might never use?",
      "Because they're buying certainty. For a few hundred dollars a year, they avoid a loss that could cost tens or hundreds of thousands, like a lawsuit after a car accident or a house fire. The best outcome is that they never file a claim, just as the best outcome for a seatbelt is never needing it. We'd keep premiums reasonable by choosing a deductible the emergency fund can cover."),
     ("How does raising a deductible save money?",
      "The client takes on the small, predictable losses themselves, so the insurer charges less. Moving from a $500 to a $1,000 deductible often cuts auto collision premiums noticeably. Over a few claim-free years, the savings usually exceed the extra $500 they'd pay in one claim. It works only if the emergency fund can cover the higher deductible, which is why we build that first.")],
    [III, NAIC])

sub("personal-insurance", "Determining personal insurance needs", ["FI:571"],
    ["Insurance needs change with life stage. A student may need only health and renters insurance. A new driver needs auto liability. A family with children needs life and disability insurance to replace income. A homeowner needs homeowners coverage. Wealthier clients add an umbrella policy for lawsuits. The goal is to cover the catastrophic risks: losing your health, your income, your property or your savings to a lawsuit.",
     "Determining needs means asking three questions: what could go wrong, how much would it cost, and could the client afford it without insurance? Life insurance need is often estimated at 10 to 12 times annual income, or calculated as debts plus future expenses (college, years of living costs) minus existing savings. Disability insurance usually aims to replace 60% to 70% of income. Health insurance protects against huge medical bills; under the Affordable Care Act, young adults can stay on a parent's plan until 26."],
    ["Term life: cheap, covers a set period (e.g. 20 years), best for most families. Whole life: lifelong coverage plus a cash value, much more expensive.",
     "Auto: liability (required in nearly every state), collision, comprehensive, uninsured/underinsured motorist; state minimums are usually far too low.",
     "Renters insurance protects belongings and liability and often costs about the price of a streaming subscription.",
     "Homeowners: dwelling, other structures, personal property, liability; flood and earthquake require separate policies.",
     "Umbrella: about $1 million of extra liability above auto and home policies, usually for a few hundred dollars a year."],
    ["About 1 in 4 of today's 20-year-olds will become disabled before reaching retirement age (Social Security Administration).",
     "About 8% of Americans had no health insurance in 2024 (U.S. Census Bureau).",
     "Standard homeowners policies don't cover flood damage; flood coverage comes from the National Flood Insurance Program or private insurers (FEMA)."],
    ["Hurricane Helene in 2024 flooded inland North Carolina towns where few homeowners carried flood insurance, leaving many with uncovered losses.",
     "A healthy 30-year-old can often buy a 20-year, $500,000 term life policy for well under $50 a month, a fraction of whole-life premiums for the same death benefit."],
    ["Term life has no cash value; whole life does.", "A homeowners policy does not cover flooding."],
    "\"With a new baby, a 20-year term life policy for about 10 times your income makes sure your child is protected until they're grown, for less than your phone bill.\"",
    [("What insurance does our young family client need right now?",
      "Four policies, in priority order. Health insurance for everyone. Term life insurance on each parent, about 10 to 12 times income, lasting until the youngest child is grown. Long-term disability coverage replacing about 60% of income, since one in four 20-year-olds will become disabled before retirement age. And renters or homeowners insurance with enough liability coverage. Together they protect the family's biggest asset: their future income."),
     ("Whole life or term life for our client?",
      "Term for almost every family. It provides the most protection per dollar: a healthy 30-year-old might pay under $50 a month for $500,000 of 20-year coverage, while whole life for the same amount can cost several hundred. The classic advice is 'buy term and invest the difference' in a Roth IRA or 401(k). Whole life can make sense for special estate-planning needs, but not as a starter policy."),
     ("How often should our client review their insurance?",
      "Once a year, and after every major life event: marriage, a new baby, buying a home, a raise or starting a business. Each one changes what they'd lose. A new home needs homeowners and maybe flood coverage. A new baby means more life insurance and naming a guardian. A quick annual review keeps coverage matched to their life.")],
    [III, HC, ["FEMA flood insurance", "https://www.floodsmart.gov"]])

sub("business-insurance", "Insurance for businesses", ["FI:571", "SM:075"],
    ["Businesses face risks that can end them overnight: a customer injury lawsuit, a fire, a data breach, a key employee's death or a shutdown that stops revenue. Business insurance transfers those risks. Small businesses often start with a business owner's policy (BOP), which bundles general liability and commercial property at a lower price, then add coverage specific to their industry.",
     "Some coverage is legally required: workers' compensation in almost every state once a business has employees, commercial auto for business vehicles, and sometimes professional licenses that require liability coverage. Lenders and landlords often require proof of insurance before they'll lend or lease. Advisors match coverage to the business's real exposures, such as product liability for a food maker or cyber coverage for anyone holding customer data."],
    ["General liability: customer injuries and property damage. Product liability: harm caused by what you sell.",
     "Commercial property: building, equipment and inventory. Business interruption: lost income and expenses during a covered shutdown.",
     "Workers' compensation: employees' medical costs and lost wages from work injuries.",
     "Professional liability (errors and omissions): advice or service mistakes, for accountants, advisors and consultants.",
     "Cyber liability: breach response, notification and lawsuits. Key person life insurance: cash if an essential owner or employee dies.",
     "Directors and officers (D&O): protects leaders against lawsuits over management decisions; fidelity bonds cover employee theft."],
    ["The average cost of a data breach was about $4.4 million globally in 2025 (IBM Cost of a Data Breach Report).",
     "Workers' compensation is required for employers in every state except Texas, where it's optional for most private employers."],
    ["In July 2024 a faulty CrowdStrike software update crashed about 8.5 million Windows computers (Microsoft), grounding flights; Delta said the outage cost it about $500 million, a reminder of third-party and business interruption risk.",
     "Many businesses learned during COVID that standard business interruption policies required physical damage, so most pandemic claims were denied."],
    ["Business interruption usually requires a covered physical loss.", "Workers' comp covers employees, not customers."],
    "\"For your food truck, we'd start with general and product liability, commercial auto and a business owner's policy. One food-poisoning claim without coverage could close the business.\"",
    [("What insurance should our small-business client buy first?",
      "Start with what could close the business. General liability for customer injuries, product liability if they sell food or goods, commercial property for equipment, and workers' compensation as soon as they hire, since it's required in almost every state. A business owner's policy bundles liability and property for less. If they store customer data, add cyber coverage, because breaches cost millions on average. Then review yearly as they grow."),
     ("How can a business lower its insurance costs without losing protection?",
      "Reduce the risk itself, then shop smart. Safety training, cameras and good record-keeping lower claims, and insurers price that in. Bundling into a business owner's policy, raising deductibles the business can afford and comparing quotes through an independent agent each year all help. A restaurant that adds anti-slip mats and fire suppression, for example, can lower both its claims and its premiums.")],
    [SBA, III])

sub("risk-process", "The risk management process and assessing an event", ["SM:075", "SM:076"],
    ["Risk is the chance that something will happen that affects goals. Risk management is the process of finding, measuring and dealing with risks so the business can still reach its goals. Pure risks can only cause loss (fire, injury); speculative risks can cause loss or gain (launching a product, investing). Risks are also grouped by source: economic, natural, human, technological, legal and reputational.",
     "The process has five steps: identify risks, assess them (how likely, how severe), prioritize, choose a response, and monitor. The four responses are avoid (don't do the risky thing), reduce or control (lower the likelihood or impact), transfer (insurance, contracts, outsourcing) and retain or accept (absorb small losses, often with a reserve fund). A risk assessment of an event, like a conference, a concert or a product launch, applies the same steps to one specific situation."],
    ["Risk matrix: rate each risk's likelihood (1-5) and impact (1-5); multiply to rank; the top-right box is handled first.",
     "Event risks: weather, crowd safety, security, vendor failure, technology failure, injury liability, low attendance, cancellation.",
     "Controls for events: permits, insurance (event cancellation, liability), vendor contracts with penalties, backup power and venue, crowd management plan, emergency procedures.",
     "Assign an owner to each risk and a trigger for acting (for example, 'if the forecast shows a 60% chance of storms 48 hours out, move indoors').",
     "After the event, review what happened and update the plan."],
    ["The 2021 Astroworld festival crowd crush killed 10 people and led to hundreds of lawsuits, showing the cost of weak crowd-safety planning.",
     "Taylor Swift's three Vienna concerts were canceled in August 2024 after authorities uncovered a terror plot; event cancellation insurance and security planning exist for exactly this."],
    ["Airlines retain the risk of small delays but transfer catastrophic aircraft liability to insurers and reinsurers.",
     "Major sports leagues buy event cancellation insurance; many policies written after 2020 now exclude pandemics."],
    ["Insurance is risk transfer, not risk avoidance.", "Assessment = likelihood x impact."],
    "\"We'd rank each risk on a simple likelihood-times-impact matrix, fix the top three first, and insure what we can't control.\"",
    [("What's the biggest risk to this plan, and what would you do about it?",
      "The biggest risk is the one with high likelihood and high impact. Here that's a cash shortfall in the first year. We'd reduce it with a three-month operating reserve, transfer part of it with a line of credit arranged before it's needed, and monitor it with a monthly cash forecast. If cash drops below two months of expenses, the trigger is to pause the expansion. Naming the risk and the trigger shows we've planned for it."),
     ("How would you assess the risks of hosting a large client event?",
      "We'd list everything that could go wrong, rate each on likelihood and impact, and plan for the top risks. For an outdoor event, weather and crowd safety rank highest, so we'd book a backup indoor space, hire licensed security, and buy liability and event cancellation insurance. Vendor contracts would include penalties for no-shows. One person owns each risk, and we review the plan 48 hours out.")],
    [COSO, SBA])

sub("erm-controls", "Enterprise risk management and internal controls", ["RM:058", "RM:062", "RM:041", "RM:042", "RM:043"],
    ["Enterprise risk management (ERM) manages risk across the whole organization, tied to strategy, instead of department by department. The COSO ERM framework (updated 2017) has leaders set a risk appetite (how much risk the company is willing to take to pursue its goals), identify and rate risks across all units, and report them to the board. It treats risk as something that can create value, not just something to avoid.",
     "Risk control means the policies and checks that keep risks in bounds. Internal controls are inside the company: approvals, segregation of duties, reconciliations, physical security, IT access limits and internal audit. External controls come from outside: regulators, laws, external auditors, credit rating agencies and contracts. COSO's internal control framework lists five components: control environment, risk assessment, control activities, information and communication, and monitoring."],
    ["Segregation of duties: the person who approves a payment shouldn't also record it or reconcile the account.",
     "Three lines model: (1) business managers own risks, (2) risk and compliance teams oversee, (3) internal audit gives independent assurance (Institute of Internal Auditors).",
     "Technology in risk management: AI and machine-learning fraud detection, real-time dashboards, cybersecurity tools, stress testing and scenario models.",
     "Legal considerations: Sarbanes-Oxley Section 404 requires public companies to assess internal controls over financial reporting; large banks face Fed stress tests; privacy and data laws set security duties.",
     "Ethics in risk management: report risks honestly, don't hide bad news, and don't let pay incentives reward excessive risk-taking."],
    ["Organizations lose an estimated 5% of revenue to fraud each year, and a lack of internal controls was the top weakness in reported cases (ACFE Occupational Fraud 2024: A Report to the Nations).",
     "Banks with $100 billion or more in assets take part in the Fed's annual stress tests (Federal Reserve)."],
    ["Wells Fargo's sales-pressure culture led employees to open millions of unauthorized accounts; the bank paid $3 billion in 2020 and operated under a Fed asset cap from 2018 until it was lifted in 2025, a lesson in incentive and conduct risk.",
     "JPMorgan's 2012 'London Whale' trades lost about $6 billion when risk limits were weakened, and the bank paid about $920 million in penalties."],
    ["Internal controls are created by the company; external controls are imposed from outside.", "ERM is organization-wide; traditional risk management works in silos."],
    "\"We'd set clear controls: two people sign off on any payment over $5,000, and someone else reconciles the account every month. It's simple, and it stops most fraud.\"",
    [("How would you prevent employee fraud in this business?",
      "With a few basic internal controls. Segregation of duties, so the person who approves payments doesn't also record them or reconcile the bank account. Dual approval for any payment over a set amount. Monthly reconciliations reviewed by the owner, and mandatory vacations so someone else covers each role. The ACFE says lack of internal controls is the top weakness in fraud cases, and these steps cost almost nothing."),
     ("What is enterprise risk management and why should our client's company use it?",
      "ERM looks at all of the company's risks together and ties them to strategy, instead of each department managing its own. Leaders set a risk appetite, rank risks across the company and report the top ones to the board. That catches risks that fall between departments. Wells Fargo's sales goals created conduct risk no single team owned, and it cost the bank $3 billion. ERM helps the company take smart risks on purpose instead of by accident.")],
    [COSO, ["ACFE Report to the Nations", "https://www.acfe.com/fraud-resources/report-to-the-nations-archive"], ["Institute of Internal Auditors", "https://www.theiia.org"]])

# =====================================================================
topic("retirement", "Retirement & estate planning", ["Financial Analysis"],
      "Retirement accounts, Social Security and Medicare, turning savings into income, and wills, trusts and passing on wealth.")

sub("retire-accounts", "Retirement accounts and the employer match", ["FI:569"],
    ["Retirement planning means saving enough during your working years to support yourself for possibly 30 years or more after you stop working. Most Americans rely on three sources: Social Security, savings in workplace plans and IRAs, and, for fewer people now, pensions. Traditional defined-benefit pensions, which promise a set monthly income, have mostly been replaced in private companies by defined-contribution plans like the 401(k), where the worker carries the investment risk.",
     "The employer match is the most powerful feature. A common formula is 50% of what you contribute up to 6% of pay, or 100% up to 3% to 5%. Not contributing enough to get the full match leaves part of your pay on the table. Vesting rules decide when the employer's money is fully yours. Self-employed people can use a SEP-IRA or Solo 401(k), and small businesses can offer a SIMPLE IRA."],
    ["A common target: save 15% of income for retirement, including the employer match.",
     "Fidelity's rough guideline: have about 10 times your final salary saved by age 67.",
     "SECURE 2.0 requires most new 401(k) plans started after 2022 to auto-enroll workers beginning in 2025, at 3%-10% of pay.",
     "Starting in 2026, workers earning more than about $150,000 must make 50+ catch-up contributions as Roth (after-tax) contributions.",
     "Leaving a job: roll the 401(k) into an IRA or the new plan; cashing out triggers taxes plus a 10% penalty before 59½."],
    ["401(k) employee limit: $24,500 for 2026 and $25,500 for 2027 (IRS Notice 2026-66).",
     "IRA limit: $7,500 for 2026 and 2027 (IRS).",
     "SECURE 2.0's Saver's Match begins in 2027: eligible lower- and moderate-income savers get a federal deposit of 50% of contributions, up to $1,000 a year, in their retirement account."],
    ["Fidelity reported record numbers of 401(k) millionaires in 2025, nearly all built by steady saving over decades rather than stock picking.",
     "General Motors, IBM and many others closed or froze traditional pensions over the past two decades, shifting retirement saving onto workers' 401(k)s."],
    ["401(k) is employer-sponsored; an IRA is opened by the individual.", "Vesting applies to employer contributions, never to your own."],
    "\"Your employer matches 100% of the first 4% you put in. Contributing less than 4% is turning down a raise.\"",
    [("How much should our client save for retirement?",
      "Aim for 15% of pay, counting the employer match, starting as early as possible. If they can't do that now, start with enough to get the full match and raise it 1% every year with each raise, so they never feel the cut. A 25-year-old earning $50,000 who saves 15% and earns about 7% could have well over $1 million by 67. The key is starting now and automating the increases."),
     ("Our client is changing jobs. What should they do with their old 401(k)?",
      "Don't cash it out. Taking $20,000 out before 59½ could cost 10% in penalty plus income tax, possibly $6,000 or more, and it loses decades of growth. Instead, roll it directly into the new employer's plan or an IRA at a low-cost brokerage, where they can pick index funds. A direct rollover avoids withholding and keeps every dollar growing for retirement.")],
    [IRS, ["DOL retirement plan basics", "https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa"]])

sub("social-security", "Social Security and Medicare", ["FI:569"],
    ["Social Security is a federal program, funded by payroll taxes, that pays retirement, disability and survivor benefits. Workers earn credits (up to four a year) and need 40 credits, about 10 years of work, to qualify for retirement benefits. The benefit is based on your highest 35 years of earnings, adjusted for wage growth. It's designed to replace about 40% of an average worker's pre-retirement income, so it's a foundation, not a full plan.",
     "When you claim matters. Full retirement age is 67 for anyone born in 1960 or later. Claiming at 62 cuts the benefit permanently by up to 30%; waiting past full retirement age adds 8% a year until 70. Benefits rise each year with a cost-of-living adjustment. Medicare, the federal health program for people 65 and older, has Part A (hospital), Part B (doctors), Part D (prescription drugs) and Medicare Advantage (Part C), private plans that bundle the others."],
    ["Check your earnings record and benefit estimate at ssa.gov/myaccount; errors can lower benefits.",
     "Waiting from 62 to 70 can raise the monthly benefit by about 75% or more.",
     "Spouses can receive up to 50% of the other spouse's full benefit; survivors can receive the deceased spouse's benefit.",
     "If you claim early and keep working, the earnings test temporarily withholds some benefits above a limit.",
     "Medicare enrollment starts three months before turning 65; signing up late for Part B can mean a permanent penalty."],
    ["Benefits rise 3.6% starting January 2027 (Social Security Administration, Oct. 2026).",
     "The 2027 earnings-test limits are $25,560 a year (under full retirement age) and $67,920 (in the year you reach it) (SSA).",
     "The 2025 Trustees Report projected the retirement trust fund would be depleted in 2033, after which incoming taxes could pay about 77% of scheduled benefits unless Congress acts (Social Security Trustees)."],
    ["More than 70 million Americans receive Social Security or SSI benefits each month (SSA).",
     "In 1983 Congress fixed a looming shortfall by raising the retirement age gradually from 65 to 67 and taxing some benefits; a similar fix is likely needed again before 2033."],
    ["Full retirement age is 67 for people born in 1960 or later, not 65.", "Medicare (65+) is different from Medicaid (low-income)."],
    "\"Social Security is a base, not the whole plan: it replaces about 40% of an average income, so your 401(k) and Roth IRA fill the rest.\"",
    [("Will Social Security still be there for young people like our client?",
      "Yes, but probably not at 100% unless Congress acts. The 2025 Trustees Report projects the retirement trust fund runs short in 2033, after which payroll taxes would still cover about 77% of promised benefits. It won't disappear, because workers keep paying in. Congress fixed a similar problem in 1983. Our plan treats Social Security as a base and builds the rest through the client's own 401(k) and Roth IRA, so they're secure either way."),
     ("When should our client start taking Social Security?",
      "If they're healthy and can afford to wait, later is usually better. Claiming at 62 permanently cuts the benefit by up to 30%, while each year after full retirement age, 67 for anyone born in 1960 or later, adds 8% until 70. Waiting from 62 to 70 can raise the monthly check by about 75%. A married higher earner who waits also gives the surviving spouse a bigger benefit. We'd use their SSA estimate and health to pick the age.")],
    [SSA, MEDICARE])

sub("retire-income", "Turning savings into retirement income", ["FI:569"],
    ["Saving is only half of retirement planning; the other half is making the money last. Retirees face longevity risk (outliving savings), inflation risk (rising costs over 30 years), market risk and sequence-of-returns risk: a market crash early in retirement does far more damage than the same crash later, because withdrawals lock in losses. A withdrawal strategy decides how much to take each year and from which accounts.",
     "A common starting point is the 4% rule from researcher William Bengen (1994): withdraw 4% of savings in year one, then adjust for inflation, and historically the money lasted at least 30 years. Retirees also combine guaranteed income (Social Security, pensions, annuities) to cover essential expenses with investments for everything else. Required minimum distributions (RMDs) force withdrawals from traditional accounts starting at age 73 (75 for people born in 1960 or later)."],
    ["Income need: many planners estimate 70% to 80% of pre-retirement income.",
     "Bucket strategy: 1-2 years of spending in cash, the next several years in bonds, the rest in stocks for growth.",
     "Withdrawal order often used: RMDs first, then taxable accounts, then traditional, then Roth (varies by tax situation).",
     "Annuities can guarantee lifetime income but may have high fees; compare carefully.",
     "Roth IRAs have no RMDs for the original owner, which helps with taxes and passing money to heirs."],
    ["A $1 million portfolio using the 4% rule supports about $40,000 a year of inflation-adjusted withdrawals.",
     "RMDs begin at 73, rising to 75 for people born in 1960 or later (IRS, SECURE 2.0)."],
    ["People who retired just before the 2008 crash and kept withdrawing heavily saw portfolios drop far more than those who could cut spending for a year or two.",
     "Employers are increasingly adding 'retirement income' options, such as built-in annuities, to 401(k) plans after SECURE Act changes."],
    ["Sequence risk is about the order of returns, not the average.", "Roth IRAs don't have RMDs during the owner's lifetime; traditional IRAs do."],
    "\"Using the 4% rule, your $800,000 would support about $32,000 a year, and with your $28,000 of Social Security that covers the $60,000 you need.\"",
    [("How do we make sure our client doesn't run out of money in retirement?",
      "Combine guaranteed income with a careful withdrawal rate. Social Security, delayed if possible, plus any pension covers essentials like housing and food. Investments cover the rest at about a 4% starting withdrawal, adjusted for inflation, which has historically lasted 30 years. Keeping one to two years of spending in cash means they never have to sell stocks in a crash. We'd review yearly and trim withdrawals after a bad market year."),
     ("Should our retiring client buy an annuity?",
      "Possibly for part of their savings, to cover essential expenses with guaranteed lifetime income. A simple immediate annuity turns a lump sum into a monthly check for life, which protects against outliving their money. But many annuities have high fees and surrender charges, so we'd compare a low-cost immediate annuity and avoid complex variable products. Covering essentials with Social Security plus a modest annuity lets the rest stay invested for growth.")],
    [IRS, SSA, INV])

sub("estate", "Estate planning", ["FI:572"],
    ["Estate planning decides what happens to your money, property and dependents if you die or become unable to make decisions. It's not just for the rich. Anyone with children, a home, retirement accounts or opinions about medical care needs a plan. The core documents are a will (who gets what, who is executor, and who becomes guardian of minor children), a durable financial power of attorney, a health care proxy and an advance directive or living will.",
     "Many assets pass outside the will. Beneficiary designations on life insurance, 401(k)s and IRAs, and 'transfer on death' or 'payable on death' labels on accounts, go straight to the named person and override the will, so they must be kept up to date. Trusts, such as a revocable living trust, can avoid probate (the public court process of settling an estate), manage money for children and keep details private. Without a will, state law decides who inherits and a court picks a guardian."],
    ["Name contingent (backup) beneficiaries on every account.",
     "Review documents after marriage, divorce, a birth or death, a move to a new state or a big change in wealth.",
     "Step-up in basis: heirs inherit assets at their value on the date of death, wiping out capital gains on growth during the owner's life.",
     "Digital assets: list accounts and passwords securely, and use each platform's legacy-contact tools.",
     "Gifts during life can reduce an estate; each person can give a set amount per recipient per year with no gift tax return."],
    ["Federal estate tax exemption: $15 million per person for 2026 and $15.48 million for 2027; annual gift exclusion: $19,000 per recipient for 2026 and $20,000 for 2027 (IRS Rev. Proc. 2026-35).",
     "Only a small fraction of estates owe federal estate tax, but every family needs a will, guardians and beneficiary designations."],
    ["Prince died in 2016 without a will; his estate took about six years and a long court fight to settle.",
     "Aretha Franklin left handwritten wills, one found in her couch cushions; a 2023 jury ruled on which one counted, years after her 2018 death."],
    ["Beneficiary designations override a will.", "Probate is public and can be slow; trusts can avoid it."],
    "\"Your 401(k) goes to whoever is named on the beneficiary form, even if your will says otherwise. Let's update it today to include your new baby as a contingent beneficiary through a trust.\"",
    [("Our clients are young parents. Why do they need estate planning now?",
      "Because the most important decision isn't about money: it's who would raise their child. Without a will, a court decides the guardian. They need a simple will naming a guardian, term life insurance with a trust or guardian to manage the money, updated beneficiaries on their 401(k)s, and powers of attorney for each other. Prince died without a will and his estate took six years to settle. A few hours now spares their family that."),
     ("What's the difference between a will and a trust?",
      "A will says who gets what and who raises minor children, but it goes through probate, a public court process that can take months or years. A revocable living trust holds assets during your life and passes them directly to heirs at death, privately and usually without probate, and can manage money for children until a set age. Many families use both: a trust for the major assets and a 'pour-over' will to catch anything left out.")],
    [IRS, ["CFPB: managing someone else's money", "https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/managing-someone-elses-money/"]])

