topic("college", "Paying for college", ["Financial Analysis"],
      "Saving for education, financial aid and the FAFSA, and student loans, with the July 2026 federal changes.")

sub("college-save", "Saving for education: 529s and other accounts", ["FI:831"],
    ["A 529 plan is a state-sponsored education savings account. Money grows without federal tax, and withdrawals for qualified education expenses are tax-free; many states also give a state tax deduction for contributions. 'Qualified' covers college tuition, fees, books, room and board, apprenticeships, up to $10,000 of student loan repayment, and K-12 tuition. Anyone can contribute, which makes it a great gift for grandparents.",
     "Other options include Coverdell education savings accounts (small limits), custodial accounts under UTMA (flexible but the child owns the money at 18 or 21), savings bonds and Roth IRAs. New since 2024, unused 529 money can roll into the beneficiary's Roth IRA, up to $35,000 over a lifetime, if the account has been open at least 15 years. Trump Accounts, created by the 2025 law, add a $1,000 federal seed for children born 2025 through 2028."],
    ["Start early: $200 a month from birth at 6% grows to about $77,000 by age 18.",
     "A 529 can be front-loaded with five years of gifts at once without gift tax.",
     "On the FAFSA, a parent-owned 529 counts as a parent asset, assessed at up to about 5.64%; grandparent-owned 529s no longer count at all.",
     "If the child gets a scholarship, you can withdraw up to the scholarship amount without the 10% penalty (earnings are still taxed).",
     "Non-qualified withdrawals: earnings are taxed plus a 10% penalty."],
    ["Starting in 2026, 529 plans can pay up to $20,000 a year of K-12 expenses, up from $10,000, and qualified expenses were expanded (2025 tax law).",
     "Trump Accounts: children who are U.S. citizens born 2025 through 2028 can get a $1,000 federal seed deposit; contributions of up to $5,000 a year began July 4, 2026 (IRS).",
     "Average published in-state tuition and fees at public four-year colleges were about $11,600 a year in 2024-25 (College Board)."],
    ["Michael and Susan Dell pledged $6.25 billion in 2025 to seed $250 into Trump Accounts for millions of children, showing how private donors are adding to the program.",
     "Many states, including New York and Virginia, give state income tax deductions for 529 contributions, which adds a return on top of growth."],
    ["529 withdrawals are tax-free only for qualified expenses.", "A custodial (UTMA) account legally belongs to the child at the age of majority."],
    "\"Starting a 529 with $200 a month now could grow to about $77,000 by the time your daughter is 18, all tax-free for college, and any leftover can roll into her Roth IRA.\"",
    [("How should new parents start saving for college?",
      "Open a 529 plan, ideally their own state's if it offers a tax deduction, and automate $100 to $200 a month. At 6%, $200 a month from birth grows to about $77,000 by 18, tax-free for qualified costs. Claim the $1,000 Trump Account seed if the baby qualifies, and ask grandparents to give to the 529 instead of toys. Retirement still comes first, because you can borrow for college but not for retirement."),
     ("What if the child doesn't go to college? Is the 529 money wasted?",
      "No, there are several good options. The 529 can pay for trade school or registered apprenticeships, switch to a sibling or cousin, or pay up to $10,000 of student loans. Since 2024, up to $35,000 over a lifetime can roll into the child's Roth IRA if the account is at least 15 years old. Only as a last resort would they withdraw it for non-education use, paying tax and a 10% penalty on the earnings only.")],
    [["Saving for college (SEC)", "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/save-for-college"], IRS])

sub("college-aid", "Financial aid, the FAFSA and student loans", ["FI:831"],
    ["Financial aid helps pay for college through grants and scholarships (free money), work-study (earned money) and loans (borrowed money). It starts with the Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA), which calculates a Student Aid Index from family income and assets and determines eligibility for Pell Grants, federal loans and work-study. Many colleges and states use it for their own aid too, and some private colleges also require the CSS Profile.",
     "Borrow federal before private. Federal loans have fixed rates, income-driven repayment options and protections that private loans lack. Subsidized loans (for students with financial need) don't charge interest while you're in school; unsubsidized loans do. A smart rule is to keep total student debt below your expected first-year salary. Choosing a lower-cost path, such as community college for two years, then transferring, in-state tuition, or generous aid packages, can cut costs dramatically."],
    ["File the FAFSA as early as possible every year; some aid is first come, first served.",
     "Compare offers by net price (cost minus grants and scholarships), not sticker price; loans are not discounts.",
     "Appeal: if family finances changed (job loss, medical bills), ask the financial aid office for a professional judgment review.",
     "Use the College Scorecard to compare graduation rates and typical earnings by school and major.",
     "Starting July 1, 2026, new Grad PLUS loans ended, new limits apply to graduate and Parent PLUS borrowing, and new borrowers repay under the new Repayment Assistance Plan or a standard plan (U.S. Department of Education)."],
    ["The 2027-28 FAFSA opened in late September 2026, its earliest launch ever (Federal Student Aid).",
     "Federal Direct loan rates for 2026-27: 6.52% undergraduate, 8.07% graduate, 9.07% Parent PLUS, fixed for the life of the loan (U.S. Department of Education).",
     "Outstanding federal student loans total about $1.7 trillion (Federal Student Aid)."],
    ["Many states offer free community college programs, such as Tennessee Promise, which covers tuition after other aid for eligible students.",
     "Student loan collections restarted in May 2025 after the pandemic pause, and many borrowers' credit scores fell when delinquencies were reported (New York Fed)."],
    ["Grants and scholarships don't have to be repaid; loans do.", "Subsidized loans don't accrue interest while you're in school; unsubsidized loans do."],
    "\"File the FAFSA the day it opens, compare schools by net price, and keep total borrowing below your expected first-year salary.\"",
    [("How can our client pay for college without drowning in debt?",
      "Layer the cheapest money first. File the FAFSA early for grants and work-study, apply for local scholarships, and compare schools by net price. Two years at community college before transferring can save tens of thousands. If they must borrow, use federal loans at 6.52% for undergraduates, and keep total debt below their expected first-year salary. That keeps payments manageable on an entry-level income."),
     ("Federal or private student loans?",
      "Federal first, almost always. Federal loans have fixed rates (6.52% for undergraduates in 2026-27), income-based repayment, deferment if they lose a job, and possible forgiveness for public service. Private loans may need a cosigner, can have variable rates and have fewer protections. Private loans should only fill a gap after federal aid, savings and scholarships, and only after comparing several lenders.")],
    [FSA, ["College Scorecard", "https://collegescorecard.ed.gov"]])

# =====================================================================
topic("bizfin", "Business finance & accounting", ["Financial Analysis", "Financial-Information Management"],
      "The role of finance in a business, the four financial statements, ratio analysis, budgets and cash flow, and how businesses raise money.")

sub("finance-role", "The role of finance and the need for financial information", ["FI:354", "FI:579", "FM:002"],
    ["Finance is the business function that gets money, manages it and puts it to the best use. The finance team, led by the chief financial officer, plans (budgets and forecasts), raises capital (loans, investors), decides which investments to make (new equipment, locations, products), manages cash and working capital, manages financial risk and reports results to owners, lenders and regulators. Every other function depends on it: marketing needs a budget, operations needs equipment and HR needs payroll.",
     "Financial information is how everyone inside and outside the business makes decisions. Managers use it to set prices, control costs and judge performance. Owners and investors use it to decide whether to invest more. Lenders use it to approve loans. Governments use it for taxes and oversight. Suppliers check it before extending credit, and employees and unions use it in pay talks. Accurate, timely information leads to better decisions; bad information leads to failures and fraud."],
    ["Key finance decisions: investing (what to buy), financing (how to pay for it), and dividend or reinvestment (what to do with profit).",
     "Capital budgeting tools: payback period, net present value (NPV) and internal rate of return (IRR). Accept projects with a positive NPV.",
     "Working capital = current assets - current liabilities; it's the cash cushion for day-to-day operations.",
     "Users of financial information: internal (managers, employees) and external (investors, lenders, government, suppliers, customers).",
     "Good information is accurate, relevant, timely, understandable and comparable.",
     "The financial-information management function collects, records, stores, analyzes and reports financial data, from bookkeeping and payroll to budgeting, audits and investor reports, so decisions rest on reliable numbers."],
    ["Public companies must file audited annual financial statements (10-K) and quarterly reports (10-Q) with the SEC (SEC).",
     "Small businesses that track finances monthly with accounting software can spot cash problems weeks earlier than those that only look at year-end taxes."],
    ["Toys R Us was bought in a 2005 leveraged buyout that loaded it with about $5 billion of debt; interest payments starved it of money to modernize, and it filed for bankruptcy in 2017.",
     "Costco's finance strategy of low markups and membership fees (most of its profit comes from fees) shows how finance shapes the whole business model."],
    ["Finance (managing money) and accounting (recording and reporting it) are related but different.", "External users need financial information too, not just managers."],
    "\"Finance turns your idea into a plan with numbers: what it costs, how we'll pay for it and when it pays back. Our projection shows break-even in month 14.\"",
    [("Why does a small business owner need good financial information?",
      "Because every decision, from pricing to hiring, depends on knowing the real numbers. Monthly statements show which products make money, when cash will run short, and whether the business can afford a new employee. Lenders will ask for them before any loan. A bakery owner who discovers that wedding cakes have a 50% margin and cupcakes only 15% can shift marketing to cakes. Good information turns guesses into decisions."),
     ("How would you decide whether the business should buy this new equipment?",
      "Compare what it costs with the cash it will bring in, adjusted for the time value of money. If a $40,000 machine saves $12,000 a year for five years, the payback is about 3.3 years, and at an 8% cost of capital the net present value is about $7,900, positive, so it adds value. We'd also check that financing it doesn't strain monthly cash flow. A positive NPV and an affordable payment means yes.")],
    [EDGAR, SBA])

sub("statements", "The four financial statements", ["FI:085", "FI:091", "FI:093", "FI:094", "FI:630"],
    ["Accounting records, classifies, summarizes and interprets a business's financial transactions. U.S. companies follow Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP), set by the Financial Accounting Standards Board; most other countries use International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS). Accrual accounting records revenue when earned and expenses when incurred, not when cash moves, which is why profit and cash can differ.",
     "Four statements tell the story. The balance sheet is a snapshot on one date: assets = liabilities + owners' equity. The income statement covers a period: revenue - expenses = net income (profit or loss). The cash flow statement shows cash in and out from operating, investing and financing activities. The statement of changes in equity (or retained earnings) explains how owners' equity moved: beginning equity + net income + new investment - dividends and buybacks = ending equity."],
    ["Balance sheet: current assets (cash, receivables, inventory), long-term assets (equipment, buildings), current liabilities (payables, short-term debt), long-term liabilities, equity.",
     "Income statement: revenue - cost of goods sold = gross profit; - operating expenses = operating income; - interest and taxes = net income.",
     "Cash flow statement: operating (day-to-day business), investing (buying or selling long-term assets), financing (loans, stock, dividends).",
     "Statement of changes in equity: shows net income added, dividends paid, stock issued or bought back.",
     "The statements connect: net income flows into equity on the balance sheet, and the cash flow statement explains the change in the cash line."],
    ["A company can show a profit and still run out of cash if customers pay slowly or inventory piles up, which is why lenders read the cash flow statement closely.",
     "Apple reported more than $400 billion in revenue for fiscal 2025 in its 10-K, and its statement of changes in equity shows tens of billions returned through buybacks and dividends (Apple 10-K)."],
    ["WorldCom recorded about $3.8 billion of ordinary expenses as assets to inflate profit; the fraud, uncovered in 2002, was one reason for the Sarbanes-Oxley Act.",
     "Enron hid debt in off-balance-sheet entities, so its balance sheet looked far healthier than it was."],
    ["The balance sheet is a point in time; the income statement covers a period.", "Depreciation reduces profit but not cash."],
    "\"Your income statement shows a $20,000 profit, but your cash flow statement shows cash fell by $8,000, because customers owe you $28,000. Collecting faster is the fix.\"",
    [("Our client's business is profitable but always short on cash. Why?",
      "Profit and cash aren't the same. Under accrual accounting, a sale counts as revenue when it's made, even if the customer pays in 60 days. If receivables and inventory are growing, cash is tied up there. The cash flow statement shows exactly where. We'd shorten payment terms, offer a 2% discount for paying within 10 days, order inventory closer to when it's needed, and arrange a line of credit for seasonal gaps."),
     ("Which financial statement would you look at first, and why?",
      "It depends on the question, but for a small business we'd start with the cash flow statement, because running out of cash is what closes businesses. Then the income statement to see whether the business model makes a profit, and the balance sheet to see what it owns and owes. Together they show whether the business is profitable, liquid and solvent. Looking at just one can be misleading.")],
    [FASB, EDGAR])

sub("ratios", "Financial analysis and analyzing customer financial information", ["FM:014", "FM:009"],
    ["Financial analysis turns statements into insight by comparing numbers to each other, to past periods (trend analysis), to competitors (benchmarking) and to the business's own plans. Ratios are the main tool. Liquidity ratios show whether bills can be paid soon; profitability ratios show how well the business turns sales into profit; leverage ratios show how much it relies on debt; efficiency ratios show how well it uses its assets.",
     "Financial services firms also analyze customers' financial information: income, debts, credit history, cash flow and assets. Banks use it to approve loans and set rates, advisors to build plans, and insurers to underwrite. Common techniques include debt-to-income ratios, credit scoring models, cash flow analysis of bank statements, net worth statements and, increasingly, AI models. These must be fair and explainable under fair-lending laws."],
    ["Current ratio = current assets / current liabilities (about 1.5 to 2 is comfortable). Quick ratio leaves out inventory.",
     "Gross margin = gross profit / revenue. Net profit margin = net income / revenue.",
     "Return on equity (ROE) = net income / equity. Return on assets (ROA) = net income / total assets.",
     "Debt-to-equity = total liabilities / equity. Times interest earned = operating income / interest expense.",
     "Inventory turnover = cost of goods sold / average inventory.",
     "For individuals: debt-to-income, savings rate, emergency-fund months, credit utilization and net worth trend."],
    ["Example: current assets $150,000 and current liabilities $100,000 give a current ratio of 1.5. Revenue $500,000 and net income $40,000 give an 8% net margin.",
     "U.S. banks' return on assets was about 1.1% to 1.4% in recent quarters, a common benchmark for bank profitability (FDIC Quarterly Banking Profile)."],
    ["Lenders such as banks and SBA lenders typically want a debt service coverage ratio of about 1.25 or higher: cash flow at least 25% above loan payments.",
     "Fintech lenders use cash flow data from bank accounts to approve 'thin-file' borrowers who have little credit history."],
    ["A high current ratio isn't always good; it can mean idle cash or slow-moving inventory.", "Ratios mean more compared to a benchmark or trend than alone."],
    "\"Your net margin is 8%, but similar shops average around 12%. That four-point gap is about $20,000 a year, and it's mostly in your food costs.\"",
    [("How would you know if this business is financially healthy?",
      "We'd check four ratios against industry benchmarks. Liquidity: a current ratio around 1.5 or more means it can pay its short-term bills. Profitability: net margin and its trend over three years. Leverage: debt-to-equity, to see if debt is manageable. Efficiency: inventory turnover. If margins are falling while debt rises, that's a warning sign, even if sales are growing. The ratios tell us where to focus first."),
     ("What information would a bank look at before lending to our client?",
      "Credit history and score, income and its stability, debt-to-income ratio, and assets or collateral, the five Cs of credit. For a business, the bank also wants two or three years of financial statements and tax returns, and a debt service coverage ratio of about 1.25 or higher. We'd prepare those documents in advance and fix any credit report errors first. Good preparation leads to a faster approval and a better rate.")],
    [["FDIC Quarterly Banking Profile", "https://www.fdic.gov/quarterly-banking-profile"], SBA])

sub("biz-budget-cash", "Business budgets and cash flow", ["FM:013", "FI:106"],
    ["A business budget is a financial plan for a future period, usually a year broken into months. It begins with a sales forecast, then builds the operating budget (expected revenue and expenses), the cash budget (when cash actually comes in and goes out), the capital budget (big purchases like equipment) and a budgeted balance sheet. Together they form the master budget. Budgets set goals, assign resources, coordinate departments and let managers spot problems early by comparing budget to actual.",
     "A static budget stays fixed; a flexible budget adjusts costs to the actual level of sales, which makes comparisons fairer. Cash flow management is where small businesses most often fail. Even profitable firms can run out of cash because of seasonal swings, slow-paying customers, too much inventory or loan payments. Tools include a rolling 13-week cash forecast, faster invoicing, early-payment discounts, negotiating longer supplier terms and a line of credit arranged before it's needed."],
    ["Cash conversion cycle = days of inventory + days of receivables - days of payables. Shorter is better.",
     "Zero-based budgeting: justify every expense from scratch each period.",
     "Keep a cash reserve of about three to six months of operating expenses.",
     "Seasonal businesses: build cash in the busy season, and use a line of credit for slow months.",
     "Budget software and accounting tools (QuickBooks, Xero) connect to bank accounts and update budget-vs-actual reports automatically."],
    ["An often-cited U.S. Bank study found that about 82% of small business failures involve cash flow problems.",
     "About half of new businesses are still operating after five years (BLS Business Employment Dynamics)."],
    ["Retailers earn a large share of annual sales in November and December, so they borrow to build inventory in the fall and repay after the holidays.",
     "Many businesses use invoice factoring, selling unpaid invoices at a discount, to get cash immediately, an expensive but fast fix."],
    ["A cash budget tracks timing of cash; an operating budget tracks revenue and expenses.", "Flexible budgets adjust to actual volume; static budgets don't."],
    "\"We'd build a 13-week cash forecast so you can see the February dip coming, and line up a $25,000 line of credit now, while your numbers look strong.\"",
    [("How would you help a seasonal business survive its slow months?",
      "Plan the cash in advance. We'd build a monthly cash budget for the year to see exactly when cash dips, then set aside part of every busy-season dollar in a reserve. We'd arrange a line of credit while the business looks strongest, not during the slump. Then cut variable costs and renegotiate supplier terms for slow months. A pool company that saves 20% of summer revenue can cover its winter payroll without borrowing."),
     ("How will you measure whether your plan is working?",
      "With a monthly budget-versus-actual report and two or three clear targets. For example: keep the cash reserve above three months of expenses, raise gross margin from 35% to 40% within six months, and cut days of receivables from 45 to 30. If any number misses by more than 10% for two months, we review and adjust. That turns the plan into something we can check, not just hope for.")],
    [SBA, SCORE])

sub("biz-funding", "How businesses raise money", ["FI:354", "EN:039"],
    ["Businesses fund themselves with debt (borrowed money that must be repaid with interest, but owners keep full ownership) or equity (selling ownership in exchange for money that doesn't have to be repaid, but owners give up a share of profits and control). Most start with bootstrapping, using the founder's savings and revenue, then friends and family, then outside sources as they grow.",
     "Debt options include bank loans, lines of credit, equipment financing, SBA-guaranteed loans (the government guarantees part of the loan, so banks lend more readily) and business credit cards. Equity options include angel investors (wealthy individuals), venture capital (funds that invest in high-growth startups for large stakes), equity crowdfunding and, for mature companies, an initial public offering. Grants and revenue-based financing fill niches. The right mix depends on the business's growth plans, risk and how much control the owner wants to keep."],
    ["Debt: interest is tax-deductible and owners keep control, but payments are due even in bad months.",
     "Equity: no required payments, but you share profits and decisions.",
     "SBA 7(a) loans go up to $5 million; SBA microloans up to $50,000 (SBA).",
     "Regulation Crowdfunding lets companies raise up to $5 million a year from ordinary investors through registered portals (SEC).",
     "Investors want a business plan, financial projections, the market size and what makes the business different."],
    ["SBA 7(a) maximum: $5 million; microloans: up to $50,000 (SBA).",
     "Reg CF crowdfunding limit: $5 million in 12 months (SEC)."],
    ["Spanx founder Sara Blakely started the company with $5,000 of her own savings and kept 100% ownership for years by bootstrapping.",
     "Many 'Shark Tank' founders turn down offers because the equity requested is worth far more than the cash, a real debt-versus-equity trade-off."],
    ["Equity holders are owners; lenders are creditors who get paid first if the business fails.", "Interest on debt is generally tax-deductible; dividends are not."],
    "\"Instead of giving up 30% of your company to an investor, an SBA microloan of $40,000 lets you keep full ownership, and the payment fits your projected cash flow.\"",
    [("Should our client take an investor's money or a bank loan to grow?",
      "Ask what they'd give up. A bank or SBA loan keeps 100% ownership, and interest is tax-deductible, but payments are due every month, so steady cash flow is essential. An investor's equity needs no repayment, but 25% of the company today could be worth millions later. For a profitable business with predictable sales, we'd recommend an SBA loan. For a high-growth idea with no cash flow yet, an investor who also brings expertise can be worth the equity."),
     ("How would you convince a lender to fund this business?",
      "Show them they'll be repaid. We'd bring a clear business plan, three years of projections with a break-even point, and the owner's credit history and personal investment, since lenders want owners to have money at stake. We'd show a debt service coverage ratio above 1.25, meaning cash flow covers loan payments with room to spare, and offer collateral or an SBA guarantee. Lenders fund preparation as much as ideas.")],
    [SBA, ["SEC crowdfunding guide", "https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/exempt-offerings/regulation-crowdfunding"], SCORE])
